  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2v(1-u-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+u}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-0-v),\\,\\frac{1-v}{1+0}) \\\\  & =(2v(1-v),\\,1-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2v(1-v),\\,y=1-v,\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=2t(1-t),\\,y=1-t,\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\nx=2(1-y)(y)\\,\u21d2\\,x=-2{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a left-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (2(1)(1-0-1),\\frac{1-1}{1+0})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (2(0)(1-0-0),\\frac{1-0}{1+0})=(0,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of a left-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(0,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/ff3c1028da35afad6c215e88863665b5bd6647d2507af2344f9f6710f0ad6373.png)",
